    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i mean, i hear what you're saying about \"conceptual rigor,\" but it feels like there's a bias in how we're defining \"lasting power\" now. it seems like the academic and gallery world has basically decided that any message is \"good\" art, even if it's visually mediocre. \n\ndoesn't that create a situation where the people who actually value technical mastery and traditional beauty are just being dismissed as \"outdated\" or \"not getting it\"? it feels like the conversation isn't even about art anymore, it's just about which political side is being represented.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are pointing to a genuine critique regarding the risk of \"conceptualism\" overshadowing visual excellence, where politics can become a substitute for craft. While some art historians agree that this can lead to a utilitarian goods crisis, the dominant narrative isn't simply about dismissing traditional beauty, but rather redefining the \"why\" of art without ignoring talent. The most respected contemporary artists now frequently balance these elements, but the tension you feel reflects the ongoing struggle to value technical skill alongside social purpose without letting ideology become the sole judge.\n</think>\n\nYou are pointing to a genuine critique regarding the risk of \"conceptualism\" overshadowing visual excellence, where politics can become a substitute for craft. While some art historians agree that this can lead to a utilitarian goods crisis, the dominant narrative isn't simply about dismissing traditional beauty, but rather redefining the \"why\" of art without ignoring talent. The most respected contemporary artists now frequently balance these elements, but the tension you feel reflects the ongoing struggle to value technical skill alongside social purpose without letting ideology become the sole judge.",
